# Docker build ignore rules for the REPO ROOT context.
#
# The web image is built from the repo root:
#
#     docker build -f web/Dockerfile .
#
# and not from web/ (see web/Dockerfile and deploy/DEPLOY.md). The web unit
# tests run as part of `npm run build` and web/src/lib/adminGrowth.test.ts reads
# the Go struct behind the /admin growth JSON at the web-relative path
# ../../../api/internal/dbstore/admin_overview.go. That cross-language read is
# the point of the guard (it pins the legend order to the API's field order
# instead of restating it), so the build context must contain api/ and the repo
# layout has to survive COPY — which means the repo root becomes the context,
# which means this file is required: without it every build ships ~1 GB to the
# daemon and `COPY . .` overwrites the node_modules that `npm ci` just created.
#
# This file applies ONLY to the root context. The api image keeps its own
# ignore file (api/.dockerignore, context api/); the root rules below do not
# weaken it.

# VCS, CI, agent worktrees and repo docs — nothing here goes into an image.
# .worktrees/ is ~600 MB in a working checkout (a worktree per Kanban card).
.git
.github
.worktrees
docs
loadtest
e2e
Makefile
AGENTS.md
README.md

# Installed dependencies and build output: the image installs its own
# (`npm ci`) and a host copy would clobber it. Also the bulk of the context.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/out
**/coverage
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
**/blob-report
**/playwright/.cache

# Go build output (api/bin, ~60 MB) — same rule as api/.dockerignore.
api/bin

# Local data, env and secrets (never ship these into an image layer).
deploy/data
*.sql.gz
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# Editor / OS noise.
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
